c  northwest for example  have a lot of morphological complexity  so a single word may be extremely long  and have a lot of smaller grammatical  units in it so treating the word as a  unit of meaning  doesn't really hold very well  cross-linguistically  not all languages have canonical  spellings or stable spelling systems um  i mean this implies a that you're using  a character based or like a phoneme  based writing system which  i i would hope that most people have  encountered or at least sort of know  about non-phoneme based writing systems  um for example  chinese mandarin  or traditional or simplified characters  are a great example of a  non-non-phonetic writing system  and many languages are used intermingled  with each other um so it is  honestly like again globally a little  weird that a lot of english speakers  just speak english and like in the same  sentence it's all english um many global  language communities will you know are  very multilingual and will use a lot of  different words and together even in the  united states spanish and english  together is very very common so a lot of  assumptions a lot of tools are built  around these assumptions and they just  aren't true  but i'm not gonna i'm not just gonna be  like  know about all languages i'm gonna give  you some orgs and resources to um kick  start the work that you might be doing  for non-english stuff um also these  slides are available on my website so  don't don't feel like you have to type  all these urls now really quickly  uh baskahane is a grassroots nlp  community run by african nlp researchers  they've done work on  i mean not every language in africa it's  a very very linguistically diverse place  but a lot of different african languages  and they are actively working on  developing tools for african languages  so it's a great group to follow if  you're working with any of those  um if you're working with spanish which  i'm guessing is going to be a little bit  more relevant for this group  hugging face which is a nlp startup  that's uh  does a lot with machine learning models  has a community specifically for spanish  and a lot of resources that they've  developed that's nlp and yes uh  iso code for spanish  uh and then the um  i'm not sorry i don't speak spanish and  i don't really want to say this bad uh  but uh the university of the republic in  uruguay has a fantastic nlp group that  has made a lot of spanish spanish  language resources available on their  github and i definitely recommend  checking that out  some of them are in python but some of  them are just sort of  programming language agnostic and you'll  be able to use in your own pipelines  and my big tip is if you are interested  in a specific language that's not you  know spoken in africa or spanish which  is what these resources are for  i would go to the acl anthology which is  the collection of all the  peer-reviewed published papers that the  acl has published the acl is the big  research conferences for nlp and you  know a great repository of knowledge and  i would search for your target language  there see who's working on it see what  they're working on  see if there's any open source tools the  nlp community the nlp research community  is pretty good about open sourcing stuff  um yeah and uh and start your search  there  so  uh things to think about  most people don't use english  cross-linguistically in the world um so  just keep that in mind uh if you are  working with the target language that  you don't speak so for me  again again that would be most of them  um hire a language consultant to work  with you on that language um sort of  standard linguistics it's really helpful  in nlp particularly when it comes to  system evaluation where you can be like  hey um or analysis evaluation being like  hey is this right  does this analysis look right to you um  and budget additional time uh so  a you know a desire that people have a  lot particularly with chatbots is to add  support for new language it's gonna take  a lot of additional time just to know  that prepare yourself here for going  into it um it's good to do but  be warned  so  those are our uh five key  mistakes that i think is pretty that are  pretty easy to make in nlp again i've  made these i know a lot about socio  linguistics and sometimes i still get  like oh no no i forgot about it so  transcribe speech is not the same as  written text think about them  differently expect variation between the  ways that people talk and the things  that they try to do um  don't do too much text cleaning like try  without text cleaning first if it  doesn't work then like do a little bit  but you know be lazy with the text  cleaning  look at your made it metadata don't  forget about languages of it in english  and then a couple well there's a couple  minutes um a couple last quick little  tips to take with you and tuck in your  shirt pocket um be a little bit paranoid  i think most data scientists are  but don't don't stop doing that uh look  at your language data at every stage  with your eyes and if you don't speak  the relevant language ask your language  consultant to look at it make sure that  you know  your assumptions about it are correct  uh do as little as possible try not to  do like big transformations that aren't  necessary particularly if you're not  using frequency-based techniques don't  feel like you have to use the  frequency-based transformations  um modular flexible pipelines keep your  new components or any sort of process  that you're doing you know  write your functions make sure that you  know you have a pipeline again general  data science advice but also true for  language um  budget for better data particularly more  relevant data if you can't find anything  in your target domain and language you  may need to like get it and it might be  expensive just know that  um  testing validation and evaluation  run your tests validate your models  evaluate your system works in practice  again just general best best practices  um and finally don't reinvent the wheel  probably someone has already done what  you are trying to do with language data  so if you want to make it work then you  can see uh what other folks have done  and again the acl anthology is a really  good place to start  or looking at a list of nlp tasks  so  uh that's all i've got thanks for  joining i know this was a little bit of  a whirlwind  very high level talk i just want you to  think about things have them in your  brain and when something seems too good  to be true  be suspicious  if you have any questions or you want to  chat more i'll be here or you can at me  on twitter thanks so much  thank you very much that was uh that was  really great  and we are going to continue with nlp in  a minute but first we are going to do a  few more giveaways
 

